# Cron ticks the pre-push deploy guard refuses to deploy on top of.
#
# THE SINGLE DECLARED SOURCE for scripts/hooks/pre-push. The hook derives
# its tick times from this file; it hardcodes nothing. tests/
# test_pre_push_hook.py reads this same file and drives the hook at times
# computed from it, so the two cannot silently disagree.
#
# These MUST mirror the Railway cron configuration, which is the real
# source of truth and lives on each service instance (Railway dashboard,
# or `railway api` -> environment.serviceInstances[].cronSchedule). They
# are NOT read from Railway at push time on purpose: a git hook that
# needs a network call to a third party fails when you are offline, and
# the repo forbids tests that touch the network, so nothing could verify
# it either.
#
# WHEN A SCHEDULE CHANGES IN RAILWAY, CHANGE IT HERE IN THE SAME COMMIT.
# Getting this wrong is not theoretical: v1.193.0 moved pricing from
# `0 6,14,22` to `25 1,6,11,16,21` and left the hook guarding the old
# times for a day -- it refused pushes at 14:00 UTC when nothing ran, and
# waved them through at 11:25 UTC when pricing was live.
#
# Format: <minute> <hours>  <service name>
# Only the two fields the guard needs are parsed. Hours may be a single
# value or a comma-separated list; `*` is not supported and is refused
# loudly, because an hourly job is a different question (see the
# CHANGELOG for v1.194.1 on why order-sync and colour-backfill are not
# guarded here).
#
# ONLY jobs whose real work runs INSIDE the app container belong here --
# those are the ones a deploy kills mid-flight. A job that runs in its own
# Railway cron service and merely calls the app over HTTP does not.

# Flow B pricing preview: background task inside the app container.
25 1,6,11,16,21   cardfoundry-cron-pricing

# Perform Sync: runs synchronously inside one HTTP request.
30 2,10,18        cardfoundry-cron-perform-sync

# Shopify quantity reconciliation: READ-ONLY, but it reads every bound
# variant inside one HTTP request (~273 Shopify calls for 6,800
# variants), so a deploy landing on it kills the run. Chosen clear of
# both jobs above by at least 35 minutes either side.
5 4,12,20         cardfoundry-cron-shopify-reconcile

# NOT GUARDED, deliberately: the Shopify order poll (hourly at :50,
# cardfoundry-cron-shopify-order-poll).
#
# ★ IT IS THE ONE CASE WHERE THIS FILE'S TWO RULES DISAGREE. Its real
# work runs synchronously inside the app container, like perform-sync,
# which is the test for belonging here -- but it is HOURLY, and hours
# must be a single value or a list, never `*`. That is not an
# oversight: an hourly job has no quiet window, so guarding it would
# refuse every push rather than protect anything. v1.194.1 decided it
# the same way for order-sync (`5 * * * *`) and colour-backfill
# (`15 * * * *`), which are also in-container HTTP jobs and are also
# not listed.
#
# ★ AND A KILLED RUN COSTS AN HOUR, NOT AN ORDER. Ingestion is keyed on
# the Shopify order id and commits per order, and the poll cursor moves
# only on a COMPLETE walk -- so a run a deploy kills leaves what it
# already ingested in place and the next tick re-reads the rest. No
# duplicate reservation, no lost order. The webhook is the fast path
# anyway; this is only the net underneath it.
#
# Moving it to a fixed hour list would make it guardable, and was
# rejected: three ticks a day means a missed webhook can sit unnoticed
# for eight hours, which is a worse failure than losing one poll run to
# a deploy.

# ALSO NOT GUARDED, deliberately: the nightly job-retention sweep
# (15 3, cardfoundry-cron-job-retention) and the vacuum that follows it
# (45 3, cardfoundry-cron-vacuum). Both do their real work in the app
# container, which is the test for belonging here -- but both are cheap,
# idempotent and daily, and each script says so in its own docstring:
# "if the app is mid-deploy, skipping tonight costs nothing", and "a
# freelist is never urgent". Named here so the absence is a decision
# somebody wrote down rather than a gap somebody will re-discover.
# (Schedules read live from Railway 2026-11-04, not assumed.)

# NOT YET IN RAILWAY, and therefore NOT GUARDED YET: the Shopify
# auto-publish (scheduled_shopify_auto_publish.py).
#
# Its real work runs synchronously inside one HTTP request in the app
# container, so it DOES belong in this file -- the moment the Railway
# service exists. It does not exist yet: v2.104.0 shipped the job with
# the service uncreated and the gate (shopify_auto_publish_enabled)
# OFF, so there is nothing running at these times to protect.
#
# ★ ADDING IT EARLY WOULD BE THE v1.193.0 BUG IN REVERSE. That release
# left the hook guarding times nothing ran at, so it refused pushes at
# 14:00 UTC for no reason. A guard on a job that does not exist is the
# same mistake, so the line below is a comment and the parser skips it.
#
# WHEN chat-shopify CREATES THE SERVICE, uncomment this in the same
# release -- the schedule here must match what is set in Railway:
#
# 40 7,12,17,22     cardfoundry-cron-shopify-auto-publish
#
# Those four ticks are each 75 minutes after a pricing run (a card is
# published once priced, and pricing is what prices it) and at least 35
# minutes clear of every other in-container tick above.
#
# ★ AND IT IS GUARDED, UNLIKE THE TWO NIGHTLY JOBS ABOVE, even though it
# is also idempotent. A killed vacuum is a no-op; a killed publish leaves
# products on Shopify that no local binding explains until the next tick
# finds them by handle and re-binds them. That is a recovery, not a
# no-op, so the run is worth not losing.
